{
  "evals": [
    {
      "id": "framework-nano-transfer-auto-joint",
      "prompt": "Run Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto) natively with Cosmos Framework on this machine's four GPUs and generate the shared daytime sample: its published seven-camera request input-transfer-auto.jsonl (joint RGB plus LiDAR, first-frame RGB conditioning, 297 frames at 30 FPS) at 480p, which the skill documents next to 720p (--resolution 480 in place of --resolution 720; the rest is the skill's reference recipe: 35 steps, guidance 3, shift 10 and the bundled per-view negative prompt assets/negative-prompt.txt). The sample is not vendored in the skill: the skill's documented workflow downloads it from the model's Hugging Face repository (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, assets/sample_data) and verifies its checksums; use that sample, no substitute media. Use the framework checkout the environment provides, the one that contains this skill (public repository https://github.com/NVIDIA/cosmos-framework.git), as the framework checkout and set it up per the skill. Put the run's sample output folder under outputs/ so that the front-wide camera video is outputs/transfer_auto/daytime_scene/vision_view01_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4, with the other six vision_view videos, sample_args.json and sample_outputs.json beside it (lidar.safetensors may stay where the run wrote it), and record in result_summary.md each camera video's resolution, frame count and fps and the tensor names, shapes and frame_ids of the generated lidar.safetensors.",
      "expected_outcome": "cosmos_framework.scripts.inference completes the published joint daytime request with Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto on four GPUs and the sample output folder (seven 832x480 camera videos of 297 frames at 30 FPS, sample_args.json and a sample_outputs.json reporting success) is saved under outputs/transfer_auto/daytime_scene/, with result_summary.md recording the exact command, the runtime, the per-camera stream facts and the generated LiDAR tensor's shapes.",
      "assertions": [
        "Inference ran through cosmos_framework.scripts.inference launched with four ranks (--nproc-per-node=4) and --cp-size 4 --cfgp-size 1 from the framework environment (not the system python), with COSMOS_TRAINING=0 and a local Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto snapshot as --checkpoint-path, on the daytime sample's input-transfer-auto.jsonl request (passed directly, or the request the native reference prepares from it)",
        "The daytime sample was downloaded from nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto (assets/sample_data) and verified against its SHA256SUMS as the skill documents, and the run used --resolution 480 with the rest of the skill's reference recipe: 35 steps, guidance 3, shift 10 and the bundled per-view negative prompt (assets/negative-prompt.txt)",
        "outputs/transfer_auto/daytime_scene/ holds seven vision_view<NN>_<camera_key>.mp4 videos, each 832x480 with 297 frames at 30 FPS, and a sample_outputs.json that reports status success; the run's lidar.safetensors holds finite range_m and intensity arrays of shape [99,128,1800] and frame_ids 0,3,...,294, as the sources state",
        "The skill's documented workflow executed without errors on the first attempt",
        "No output was fabricated: the camera videos and the LiDAR facts trace to a real inference run in the transcript",
        "result_summary.md contains the exact command line used, the measured runtime and the stream and tensor facts actually observed"
      ],
      "checks": [
        {
          "path": "outputs/transfer_auto/daytime_scene/vision_view01_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4",
          "min_bytes": 200000
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/transfer_auto/daytime_scene/sample_outputs.json",
          "contains": "success"
        },
        {
          "path": "result_summary.md",
          "min_bytes": 150
        },
        {
          "path": "transcript.md",
          "min_bytes": 300
        }
      ],
      "stages": [
        "framework setup",
        "sample + checkpoint download",
        "inference run",
        "verify + summarize"
      ],
      "gpus": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "vllm-omni-nano-transfer-auto-wsm",
      "prompt": "Serve nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto with vLLM-Omni, launched in the two-GPU form its reference documents (Docker's --gpus all and --cfg-parallel-size 2), and run the WSM-only example of the shared daytime sample through the server's synchronous Videos API, the way the skill's vLLM-Omni reference for this model does: the request template assets/daytime_scene/transfer_auto_wsm.json (seven WSM controls with their per-camera captions and no RGB conditioning, 201 frames per camera at 30 FPS, 832x480) plus the bundled per-view negative prompt assets/negative-prompt.txt. The sample is not vendored in the skill: the skill's documented workflow downloads it from the model's Hugging Face repository (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, assets/sample_data) and verifies its checksums; use that sample, no substitute media. Use the framework checkout the environment provides, the one that contains this skill (public repository https://github.com/NVIDIA/cosmos-framework.git), as the checkout the client runs in. Confirm the server serves the model before the request, then put the example's output folder under outputs/ so that the server's camera-major response is outputs/vllm_omni/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4, with the seven per-camera videos (vision_view00_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4 first), request.json and sample_outputs.json beside it (the prepared_controls folder need not be copied). Record the response's resolution, frame count and fps, the time-to-ready and the request latency in result_summary.md, then stop the server.",
      "expected_outcome": "The vLLM-Omni server comes up serving nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, one synchronous WSM-only request returns a camera-major MP4 of 7 x 201 = 1407 frames at 832x480 and 30 FPS that is split into seven per-camera videos, and the output folder plus a summary with server, readiness and latency details exist in artifacts.",
      "assertions": [
        "The vLLM-Omni server was started from the vllm/vllm-omni:cosmos3 image with the documented command for nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto in its documented two-GPU form (--gpus all and --cfg-parallel-size 2; --omni --model-class-name Cosmos3MultiviewPipeline, --diffusion-compile-granularity regional --no-diffusion-compile-dynamic, --no-guardrails, VLLM_OMNI_VIDEO_SYNC_TIMEOUT=1800 and VLLM_OMNI_DLO_DP_WAVE_TIMEOUT=1800) and GET /v1/models returned the model before the generation request",
        "The request went to POST /v1/videos/sync as a multipart form with the seven prepared WSM controls uploaded as input_references and extra_params carrying wsm, the multiview views (camera_key, prompt, control_reference_index) and per_view_negative_prompt, built from assets/daytime_scene/transfer_auto_wsm.json (201 frames, 30 FPS, seed 0, 35 steps, guidance 3, flow shift 10) and the daytime sample downloaded from nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto and verified against its SHA256SUMS",
        "outputs/vllm_omni/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4 is the server's response: a valid MP4 of 7 x 201 = 1407 frames at 832x480 and 30 FPS, split into seven per-camera videos of 201 frames each, with sample_outputs.json recording status success",
        "The skill's documented workflow executed without errors on the first attempt",
        "No output was fabricated: the videos trace to a real server response in the transcript",
        "result_summary.md records the server command, time-to-ready, request latency and the stream facts actually observed, and the transcript shows the server was stopped at the end"
      ],
      "checks": [
        {
          "path": "outputs/vllm_omni/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4",
          "min_bytes": 200000
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/vllm_omni/auto/wsm/vision_view00_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4",
          "min_bytes": 50000
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/vllm_omni/auto/wsm/sample_outputs.json",
          "contains": "success"
        },
        {
          "path": "result_summary.md",
          "min_bytes": 150
        },
        {
          "path": "transcript.md",
          "min_bytes": 300
        }
      ],
      "stages": [
        "server start",
        "client setup + sample download",
        "readiness",
        "generation request",
        "validate + teardown + summarize"
      ],
      "docker": true,
      "gpus": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "sglang-nano-transfer-auto-wsm",
      "prompt": "Serve nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto with SGLang and run the WSM-only example of the shared daytime sample as one asynchronous Videos API job, the way the skill's SGLang reference for this model does: the request template assets/daytime_scene/transfer_auto_wsm.json (seven WSM controls with their per-camera captions and no RGB conditioning, 201 frames per camera at 30 FPS, 832x480) plus the bundled per-view negative prompt assets/negative-prompt.txt, with the prepared controls read by the server from their paths (nothing is uploaded). The sample is not vendored in the skill: the skill's documented workflow downloads it from the model's Hugging Face repository (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, assets/sample_data) and verifies its checksums; use that sample, no substitute media. Use the framework checkout the environment provides, the one that contains this skill (public repository https://github.com/NVIDIA/cosmos-framework.git), as the checkout whose .agents/skills folder the server mounts. Confirm the server serves the model before the request, poll the job until it completes, then copy the example's results out of its output folder (which stays inside the mounted .agents/skills, as the reference requires) to outputs/ so that the downloaded camera-major video is outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4, with the seven per-camera videos (vision_view00_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4 first), request.json, job.json and sample_outputs.json beside it (the prepared_controls folder need not be copied). Record the video's resolution, frame count and fps, the time-to-ready and the job latency in result_summary.md, then stop the server.",
      "expected_outcome": "An SGLang-served Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto completes one asynchronous WSM-only job from server-readable control paths, the downloaded camera-major MP4 of 7 x 201 = 1407 frames at 832x480 and 30 FPS is split into seven per-camera videos, and the output folder, summary and transcript are in artifacts.",
      "assertions": [
        "The SGLang server was started from the lmsysorg/sglang:dev image (or the fixed tag the reference names for its validated run) with the documented sglang serve command for nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, with the cookbook root (.agents/skills) mounted at the same absolute path inside the container, and GET /v1/models returned the model before the request",
        "The request was built from assets/daytime_scene/transfer_auto_wsm.json (201 frames, 30 FPS, seed 0, 35 steps, guidance 3, flow shift 10) with prepare_request(..., backend=\"sglang\"): multipart form fields with no file uploads, each camera's absolute control_path and the per_view_negative_prompt inside extra_params; the job was submitted to POST /v1/videos, polled to completion and the MP4 downloaded from its /content route",
        "outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4 is the downloaded result: a valid MP4 of 7 x 201 = 1407 frames at 832x480 and 30 FPS, split into seven per-camera videos of 201 frames each and recorded in sample_outputs.json",
        "The skill's documented workflow executed without errors on the first attempt",
        "No output was fabricated: the daytime sample came from nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto (assets/sample_data), verified against its SHA256SUMS, and the videos trace to a real completed job in the transcript and job.json",
        "result_summary.md records the server command, time-to-ready, job latency and the stream facts actually observed, and the transcript shows the server was stopped at the end"
      ],
      "checks": [
        {
          "path": "outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/multiview_camera_major.mp4",
          "min_bytes": 200000
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/vision_view00_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4",
          "min_bytes": 50000
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/sample_outputs.json",
          "contains": "camera_major_response"
        },
        {
          "path": "outputs/sglang/auto/wsm/job.json",
          "contains": "completed"
        },
        {
          "path": "result_summary.md",
          "min_bytes": 150
        },
        {
          "path": "transcript.md",
          "min_bytes": 300
        }
      ],
      "stages": [
        "server start",
        "client setup + sample download",
        "readiness",
        "generation job",
        "validate + teardown + summarize"
      ],
      "docker": true,
      "gpus": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "framework-nano-sim-auto-joint",
      "prompt": "Run Cosmos3-Nano-Sim-Auto (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Sim-Auto), the four-step causal student, natively with Cosmos Framework on this machine's four GPUs and generate the shared daytime sample: its published seven-camera request input-sim-auto.jsonl (joint RGB plus LiDAR, first-frame RGB conditioning, 297 frames at 30 FPS, 720p) with the student's own settings (guidance 1, control guidance 1 and the checkpoint's four-step schedule). The sample is not vendored in the skill: the skill's documented workflow downloads it from the Transfer-Auto model's Hugging Face repository (nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto, assets/sample_data), which publishes it for both Auto models, and verifies its checksums; use that sample, no substitute media. Use the framework checkout the environment provides, the one that contains this skill (public repository https://github.com/NVIDIA/cosmos-framework.git), as the framework checkout and set it up per the skill. Put the run's sample output folder under outputs/ so that the front-wide camera video is outputs/sim_auto/daytime_scene/vision_view01_camera_front_wide_120fov.mp4, with the other six vision_view videos, caption_chunks.json, rolling_prompt.json, sample_args.json and sample_outputs.json beside it (lidar.safetensors, lidar.pt and the control videos may stay where the run wrote them), and record in result_summary.md each camera video's resolution, frame count and fps and the tensor names and shapes of the generated lidar.safetensors.",
      "expected_outcome": "cosmos_framework.scripts.inference completes the published joint daytime request with Cosmos3-Nano-Sim-Auto on four GPUs and the sample output folder (seven 1280x720 camera videos of 297 frames at 30 FPS, caption_chunks.json, rolling_prompt.json, sample_args.json and a sample_outputs.json reporting success) is saved under outputs/sim_auto/daytime_scene/, with result_summary.md recording the exact command, the runtime, the per-camera stream facts and the generated LiDAR tensor's shapes.",
      "assertions": [
        "Inference ran through cosmos_framework.scripts.inference launched with four ranks (--nproc-per-node=4) and --cp-size 4 --cfgp-size 1 from the framework environment (not the system python), with COSMOS_TRAINING=0 and a local Cosmos3-Nano-Sim-Auto snapshot as --checkpoint-path, on the daytime sample's input-sim-auto.jsonl request (passed directly, or the request the native reference prepares from it)",
        "The run kept the student's settings: guidance 1, control guidance 1 and the checkpoint's four-step schedule, with no Transfer-Auto steps, shift or negative-prompt setting added",
        "outputs/sim_auto/daytime_scene/ holds seven vision_view<NN>_<camera_key>.mp4 videos, each 1280x720 with 297 frames at 30 FPS, plus caption_chunks.json, rolling_prompt.json and a sample_outputs.json that reports status success; the run's lidar.safetensors holds finite range_m and intensity arrays of shape [99,128,1800] (99 sweeps at 10 Hz) with frame_ids, as the sources state",
        "The skill's documented workflow executed without errors on the first attempt",
        "No output was fabricated: the daytime sample came from nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano-Transfer-Auto (assets/sample_data), verified against its SHA256SUMS, and the camera videos and the LiDAR facts trace to a real inference run in the transcript",
        "result_summary.md contains the exact command line used, the measured runtime and the stream and tensor facts actually observed"
